e solution to each subproblem, where <code>res[i]</code> contains the solut=
ion to the subproblem of an <code>i</code>-person circle. Using this array,=
 our formula becomes:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><code>res[i] =3D (res[i - 1] + k) % i</code></p>
</blockquote>
<p>The key observation here is that we only ever need access to the previou=
s element of the <code>res</code> array (at <code>i - 1</code>) to calculat=
e the result of the current subproblem (at <code>i</code>). This means we d=
on=E2=80=99t need to store the entire array.</p>
<p>Instead, we can use a single variable to keep track of the solution to t=
he previous subproblem. We can then update this variable to store the solut=
ion for the current subproblem:</p>
<blockquote>
<p><code>res =3D (res + k) % i</code></p>
</blockquote>
<p>Note that the 'res' value used on the right-hand side of the equation re=
presents the previous subproblem=E2=80=99s result.</p>
